{"name":"Grange Hall","hex":"#857767","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"grange","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A granary.","A farm, with its associated buildings; a farmhouse, particularly one associated with a monastic order, or manor."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/grange"},{"term":"hall","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A corridor; a hallway.","A large meeting room."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/hall"}],"entries":[{"title":"Grange Hall (Wilton, Connecticut)","blocks":[{"text":"The Grange Hall in the Cannondale section of the town of Wilton, Connecticut is a historic Grange building, and is home of the Cannon Grange."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a6/Cannon_Grange_Hall.jpg/330px-Cannon_Grange_Hall.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Grange_Hall_(Wilton%2C_Connecticut)","wikidata":"Q5595653"},{"title":"Grange Hall (Murphysboro, Illinois)","blocks":[{"text":"The Grange Hall in Somerset Township, Jackson County, Illinois, is the historic meeting place of Somerset Township's chapter of The Grange. Built in 1912, the building was Somerset Grange #1553's second meeting hall; the first building was built in 1876 and burned down in 1909. The red brick building was built by contractor W. A. Pitman in the Commercial style. The Grange Hall served as a meeting place for local farmers to discuss agricultural affairs and propose farm policy to legislators. The National Farmers Union's newspaper, the Union Farmer, was published from the Somerset Grange Hall until 1914. The building also served as a local social center and hosted township elections, club meetings, and community events. The hall was rehabilitated in 1988; it still serves as a township polling place."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cd/Grange_hall_in_Somerset_Township.jpg/330px-Grange_hall_in_Somerset_Township.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Grange_Hall_(Murphysboro%2C_Illinois)","wikidata":"Q5595651"},{"title":"National Grange","blocks":[{"text":"The National Grange, also known as The Grange and officially named The National Grange of the Order of Patrons of Husbandry, is a social organization in the United States that encourages families to band together to promote the economic and political well-being of the community and agriculture. The Grange, founded after the Civil War in 1867, is the oldest American agricultural advocacy group with a national scope. The Grange actively lobbied state legislatures and U.S. Congress for political goals, such as the Granger Laws to lower rates charged by railroads, and rural free mail delivery by the U.S. Post Office."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3e/Gift_for_the_grangers_ppmsca02956u.jpg/330px-Gift_for_the_grangers_ppmsca02956u.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/National_Grange","wikidata":"Q7753183"},{"title":"Hall","blocks":[{"text":"In architecture, a hall is a relatively large space enclosed by a roof and walls. In the Iron Age and the Early Middle Ages in northern Europe, a mead hall was where a lord and his retainers ate and also slept. Later in the Middle Ages, the great hall was the largest room in castles and large houses, and where the servants usually slept. As more complex house plans developed, the hall remained a large room for dancing and large feasts, often still with servants sleeping there. It was usually immediately inside the main door. In modern British houses, an entrance hall next to the front door remains an indispensable feature, even if it is essentially merely a corridor."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2b/Great_Mosque_of_Kairouan_prayer_hall.jpg/330px-Great_Mosque_of_Kairouan_prayer_hall.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hall","wikidata":"Q240854"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8a/Grange.jpg/330px-Grange.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8a/Grange.jpg/330px-Grange.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Grange Hall in Solon, Maine c. 1910","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AGrange.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/76/Stamp-national_grange.jpg/500px-Stamp-national_grange.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/76/Stamp-national_grange.jpg/500px-Stamp-national_grange.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"1967 U.S. postage stamp honoring the National Grange","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AStamp-national_grange.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/6/66/Slatersville_Grange.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 1x","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/6/66/Slatersville_Grange.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","alt":"Union Grange Hall in Slatersville, Rhode Island, now a community center belonging to the North Smithfield Heritage Association.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASlatersville_Grange.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e4/Grange1873.jpg/330px-Grange1873.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e4/Grange1873.jpg/330px-Grange1873.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=pa